<h2>The conviction</h2>
<p>The applicant was convicted by the Special Judge (NDPS cases), Hanumangarh in Sessions Case No. 02 of 2020 under Sections 8/22 of the NDPS Act — possession of a psychotropic substance in a quantity below the commercial threshold — and sentenced to twenty years. His appeal is pending before the High Court; with no early hearing in prospect, he moved a second application for suspension of sentence.</p>

<h2>The standard the Court applied</h2>
<p>The order draws the line that post-conviction liberty jurisprudence requires. Suspension of sentence is not bail re-argued: the presumption of innocence is gone, and sympathy is not a ground. What the appellate court must instead do is read the memo of appeal against the judgment under challenge: <em>&ldquo;the appellate court is required to evaluate whether the grounds raised in appeal disclose a substantial and arguable case.&rdquo;</em> Where they do, the second variable enters — time. Relying on <strong>Kashmira Singh</strong>, the Court held that <em>&ldquo;continued incarceration, despite arguable grounds in appeal, would not be justified&rdquo;</em> when the appeal itself cannot realistically be heard soon. A convict who ultimately succeeds after serving a decade of a sentence that should never have been imposed cannot be compensated by the acquittal.</p>

<h2>Where NDPS cases fit</h2>
<p>NDPS convictions add one structural question: quantity. Commercial-quantity cases attract Section 37&rsquo;s twin conditions, which continue to cast a shadow even at the suspension stage; below-commercial cases — like this one — are governed by the general Kashmira Singh calculus. Within that calculus, the long sentences the NDPS Act prescribes cut in the convict&rsquo;s favour on the time factor: the longer the sentence and the slower the docket, the greater the fraction of the sentence that will stand served before the appeal is decided.</p>
